# Work levels

Classify the work, then continue. [ADR 004](../../docs/adr/004-agent-work-lanes.md) is why. You choose the steps inside a level.

| Level | Model | Where it runs |
|-------|--------|----------------|
| Design and architecture | This chat's intelligent model | Here |
| Implementation of an accepted design, and bug fixes | `composer-2.5` | Subagent |
| Chores (git, tests, validation, translation, formatting) | `composer-2.5-fast` | Subagent |

Launch a general-purpose subagent and pass that `model`. Hand it the goal, the decision, and the files. Let it decide the steps.

Design stays here: trade-offs, shape, and the words in `docs/adr/` and `CONTEXT.md`. A settled design is what implementation receives.

Commits use a conventional prefix (`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`) and a subject that says why.

A cheaper run that would change a product decision comes back here. You decide. Record it in `docs/adr/` when it is hard to reverse, surprising, and a real trade-off. Product terms go in `CONTEXT.md`.

`mvp-demo-host` and `mvp-demo-fe` run only when the user names them.
